Subject: [PATCH] LOCK CMPXCHG8B on an address that is not 8-byte aligned is atomic

Where CASP cannot do the 8 bytes (without LSE2 any address that is not 8-byte aligned, with LSE2
8 bytes across a 16-byte boundary), the dynarec read the 8 bytes with a plain load, compared them
under a byte exclusive, and wrote them with a plain store after the exclusive store; the
interpreter did the same. A second CPU's compare-exchange could land between the exclusive store
and the plain store (or between the plain load and the exclusive load) and be lost. 32-bit x86
.NET aligns objects to 4 bytes and changes their 64-bit fields with this instruction
(Interlocked.CompareExchange on a long).

Both now call arm64_lock_cmpxchg8b_unaligned (dynarec_arm64_functions.c), so they agree for each
address:
- 8 bytes inside one 16-byte block: an exclusive pair (LDAXP/STLXP) on the whole block; a failed
  compare writes the same bytes back, which makes the read atomic.
- 8 bytes across a 16-byte boundary: one lock for all such compare-exchanges (atomic with each
  other, not with other instructions on the same bytes); under WowBox64 the thread holds no
  suspend signal while it holds it (WowHoldEnter, mypthread.h).
- A barrier before and after: a full barrier, as an x86 locked instruction is.
